From: Nuxel <nuxel@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 0029] regulator: fix regulator_force_enable() open_count corruption

The 0028 regulator_force_enable() copied the trailing
"while (rdev->open_count--) regulator_enable(rdev->supply)" tail from
regulator_force_disable().  For enable this is wrong:

1. It drains rdev->open_count to zero, corrupting the consumer-handle
   invariant (teardown later hits WARN_ON(rdev->open_count)).
2. It enables the PARENT supply open_count times outside any lock, which
   diverges from upstream regulator_enable() ordering and count semantics.

_regulator_do_enable() already performs ena_gpio_ctrl/ops->enable plus the
ramp delay; it is the same physical primitive _regulator_enable() uses.  The
correct symmetric enable for the bounded recovery path (immediately following
regulator_force_disable()) does not touch open_count and does not recurse into
the supply chain.

Signed-off-by: Nuxel <nuxel@localhost>
---
diff --git a/drivers/regulator/core.c b/drivers/regulator/core.c
index 093f02c88e00..14eac61f0f56 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/core.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/core.c
@@ -2420,10 +2420,6 @@ int regulator_force_enable(struct regulator *regulator)
 	ret = _regulator_do_enable(rdev);
 	mutex_unlock(&rdev->mutex);
 
-	if (rdev->supply)
-		while (rdev->open_count--)
-			regulator_enable(rdev->supply);
-
 	return ret;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(regulator_force_enable);
